WEBVTT

00:00:01.50 --> 00:00:06.56
This is journey twenty seven of Hewn, the software factory: reconnect Mission

00:00:06.56 --> 00:00:07.09
Control.

00:00:07.09 --> 00:00:08.01
Networks drop.

00:00:08.01 --> 00:00:13.46
This video shows Mission Control noticing, saying so, retrying on a fixed schedule,

00:00:13.46 --> 00:00:16.68
and catching up every event it missed, once each,

00:00:16.68 --> 00:00:20.10
so the screen ends up as a fresh load would show it.

00:00:21.25 --> 00:00:25.77
When the live connection drops, Mission Control shows Reconnecting,

00:00:25.77 --> 00:00:31.09
and retries after zero, two, ten and thirty seconds, then every thirty seconds.

00:00:31.09 --> 00:00:36.69
On reconnect it fetches every event after its last sequence number and applies each

00:00:36.69 --> 00:00:37.02
once.

00:00:37.02 --> 00:00:39.72
When catch-up finishes, the banner goes.

00:00:39.72 --> 00:00:44.57
This is for everyone watching Mission Control, Owners and Viewers alike.

00:00:45.72 --> 00:00:47.25
Catch-up already worked.

00:00:47.25 --> 00:00:51.97
Every event carries a per-project sequence number, one more than the last,

00:00:51.97 --> 00:00:57.27
and on reconnect the client asked for everything after the last one it had applied.

00:00:57.27 --> 00:01:00.71
But nothing on screen said the connection had dropped,

00:01:00.71 --> 00:01:05.44
and SignalR's automatic reconnect tried six times in thirty three seconds,

00:01:05.44 --> 00:01:06.84
then gave up for good.

00:01:07.99 --> 00:01:11.15
Here a project is running, and its log is live.

00:01:11.15 --> 00:01:12.36
The network drops.

00:01:12.36 --> 00:01:17.60
A strip appears above the page: Reconnecting, the next attempt in two seconds,

00:01:17.60 --> 00:01:21.77
and the sequence number that missed events will catch up from.

00:01:21.77 --> 00:01:25.67
The sidebar says Reconnecting too, with a dot that pulses,

00:01:25.67 --> 00:01:28.02
so the state is never colour alone.

00:01:28.37 --> 00:01:31.94
The worker keeps going, but nothing new reaches the page.

00:01:31.94 --> 00:01:35.51
At the attempt twelve seconds in, the connection is back.

00:01:35.51 --> 00:01:40.65
The strip says what it caught up: twelve missed events, numbers ten to twenty one.

00:01:40.65 --> 00:01:43.66
The pipeline jumps ahead to where the worker is,

00:01:43.66 --> 00:01:46.23
and five seconds later the strip is gone.

00:01:47.38 --> 00:01:50.77
A small class, the reconnector, owns the schedule.

00:01:50.77 --> 00:01:53.68
It waits zero, two, ten and thirty seconds,

00:01:53.68 --> 00:01:56.67
then thirty seconds for as long as it takes.

00:01:56.67 --> 00:02:01.07
After the four scheduled attempts, the connection counts as lost.

00:02:01.42 --> 00:02:06.64
Each attempt starts the connection, then every open subscription joins its group

00:02:06.64 --> 00:02:08.01
again and catches up.

00:02:08.01 --> 00:02:13.17
Only then does the status turn live, so the banner goes when catch-up finishes,

00:02:13.17 --> 00:02:14.86
not when the socket opens.

00:02:15.21 --> 00:02:18.25
Here, on a fake clock, the schedule runs out.

00:02:18.25 --> 00:02:22.71
The strip turns to Connection lost: retrying every thirty seconds,

00:02:22.71 --> 00:02:25.41
agents keep working and nothing is lost.

00:02:25.41 --> 00:02:27.03
Retry now tries at once.

00:02:27.03 --> 00:02:31.68
When the network is back, it reconnects, and says nothing was missed.

00:02:32.03 --> 00:02:35.91
The banner's text is one small function of the status, the time,

00:02:35.91 --> 00:02:37.30
and what was caught up.

00:02:38.45 --> 00:02:43.40
When the first connection is slow, the strip says Mission Control is connecting,

00:02:43.40 --> 00:02:45.56
and live updates start in a moment.

00:02:45.56 --> 00:02:48.03
A quick connection shows nothing at all.

00:02:49.18 --> 00:02:52.33
The reconnector's unit tests use fake timers.

00:02:52.33 --> 00:02:55.55
Attempts land at zero, two, twelve, forty two,

00:02:55.55 --> 00:02:58.49
seventy two and a hundred and two seconds.

00:02:58.49 --> 00:03:01.78
Retry now tries at once and keeps the schedule.

00:03:01.78 --> 00:03:03.88
Banner text has its own tests.

00:03:03.88 --> 00:03:08.15
The front end has a hundred and sixty five unit tests in all.

00:03:08.50 --> 00:03:13.27
End to end, a page object routes the hub's connection through Playwright,

00:03:13.27 --> 00:03:16.28
so a test can cut it and refuse every attempt.

00:03:16.28 --> 00:03:19.81
A behaviour test steps the clock through the schedule,

00:03:19.81 --> 00:03:22.24
Retry now and the Reconnected notice.

00:03:22.59 --> 00:03:26.22
A pipeline test cuts the connection while a worker logs.

00:03:26.22 --> 00:03:28.36
Nothing arrives while it is down.

00:03:28.36 --> 00:03:33.42
After the reconnect, the pipeline and the log match a fresh page load exactly.

00:03:33.42 --> 00:03:38.61
Against the old Mission Control, both tests failed: there was no banner to find.

00:03:38.61 --> 00:03:42.83
Visual tests match the connecting and lost strips to their mocks,

00:03:42.83 --> 00:03:45.75
and an accessibility test checks every state.

00:03:46.90 --> 00:03:50.10
The strip reads the same in dark mode, and on a phone.

00:03:51.25 --> 00:03:53.67
Journey twenty seven is complete.

00:03:53.67 --> 00:03:57.49
Mission Control says when its live connection drops,

00:03:57.49 --> 00:04:01.38
retries on L2-032's schedule for as long as it takes,

00:04:01.38 --> 00:04:06.80
catches up every missed event once, and clears the banner when it is done.

00:04:06.80 --> 00:04:09.15
Next comes journey twenty eight.
